<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Donation of Pepin</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/756">756</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Donation of Pepin was the grant by the Frankish king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepin-the-short">Pepin the Short</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a> of lands in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> taken from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombards</a>, promised in 754 and carried out in 756. It gave the popes the former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/exarchate-of-ravenna">Byzantine Exarchate of Ravenna</a> and other territories, forming the legal basis of the Papal States, and sealed the alliance between the papacy and the Frankish kings.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/donation-of-pepin.jpg" alt="Donation of Pepin" width="320" height="308" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: English: A fresco painter from the school of Giovan Battista Ricci (c. 1537–1627) … · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:La_donacion_de_Pipino_el_Breve_al_Papa_Esteban_II.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>In 751 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aistulf">Aistulf</a>, king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombards</a>, conquered what remained of the exarchate of Ravenna, the last vestige of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. In 752 he demanded the submission of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and a tribute of one gold <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solidus">solidus</a> per head. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-stephen-ii">Pope Stephen II</a> tried negotiations and bribes, and when they failed, he led a procession through Rome and nailed the violated treaty to a crucifix. He appealed to the Byzantine emperor Constantine V without success, then to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepin-the-short">Pepin the Short</a>. Stephen was refused by Aistulf at Pavia and became the first pope to cross the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a>, reaching Pepin's estate at Ponthion on 6 January 754.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>At Ponthion, the pope in sackcloth and ashes asked Pepin to support the suit of St Peter and the republic of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>, and Pepin promised to restore the exarchate of Ravenna and the rights and territories of the republic. In April 754 an assembly at Quierzy-sur-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oise">Oise</a> heard him restate the promise and list the territories, and some noblemen left in opposition. The promise was afterwards put in writing. On 28 July 754 the pope anointed Pepin and his sons Charles and Carloman as kings of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> and patricians of the Romans.</p><p>In spring 755 the Frankish army crossed Mont Cénis and defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombards</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/susa">Susa</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aistulf">Aistulf</a> swore to return Ravenna. As soon as the army left, he broke the treaty and put <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> under siege on 1 January 756. After three months Aistulf lifted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naval-blockade">blockade</a>, and in April a Frankish army again defeated the Lombards. Aistulf gave hostages, agreed to pay annual tribute and promised in writing to return the lands.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The treaty of 756 conferred on the pope the territories of Ravenna, including Forlì, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a> conquests in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romagna">Romagna</a> and in the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spoleto">Spoleto</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benevento">Benevento</a>, and the Pentapolis of Rimini, Pesaro, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fano">Fano</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sena-gallica">Senigallia</a> and Ancona. Envoys of the Empire offered Pepin money to have the lands returned, but he refused, saying they belonged to St Peter. Abbot Fulrad collected the keys of the cities and laid them with the written agreement on the tomb of St Peter. The boundaries can only be approximated, since the text has not survived.</p><p>The Donation made the pope a temporal ruler for the first time, over a strip of territory running across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> from the Tyrrhenian to the Adriatic, though the popes could not exercise effective <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> over it. Pepin's successors <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-pious">Louis the Pious</a> confirmed it in 778 and 817. Scholars debate the terminology, since the lands were technically not Pepin's to give.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Donation of Pepin” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Pepin defeated the Lombards in 754 and 756 and gave the pope the lands of the former Exarchate of Ravenna, the so-called <b>Donation of Pepin</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/papal-states">Papal States · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
